      "worked": "The original denominator requires $x\\ne1$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x^4-1&=(x^2-1)(x^2+1)\\\\x^4-1&=(x-1)(x+1)(x^2+1).\\end{align*}$$\n\nFor $x\\ne1$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)(x^2+1)}{x-1}\\\\y&=(x+1)(x^2+1).\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $x^2+1>0$, the only zero is $x=-1$, giving the $x$-intercept $(-1,0)$.\n\nAt $x=0$,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=(0+1)(0^2+1)\\\\y&=1.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $(0,1)$. At $x=1$, the simplified cubic would have height $(1+1)(1^2+1)=4$. The original expression is undefined there, so mark an open circle at $(1,4)$.\n\nSketch the increasing cubic from lower left to upper right through the intercepts, leaving the hole at $(1,4)$. There is no vertical asymptote at $x=1$.",
